16.2 The Cost Model
A cost driver is any factor that affects total firm costs [Niraj et al., 2001].
Typically, costs vary over time or volume of output. The four primary types
of cost drivers are: fixed, variable, semivariable, and nonrecurring. Fixed costs
do not vary at all with volume. Examples include rent and management sala-
ries. Variable costs change directly and proportionally with volume. Examples
include sales commissions (which vary with sales) and materials costs (which
vary with the amount of goods produced). Semivariable costs also change with
volume, though not as directly and they contain elements of fixed costs. For
example, a store may need to keep a minimum staff on hand (which is a fixed
cost), but can add more staff as the number of customers increases (which is
a variable cost). Finally, nonrecurring costs occur infrequently or irregularly.
Examples include equipment and building purchases [Hamermesh et al., 2002].
In order to maximize profits, entrepreneurs must be aware of their various cost
drivers and of the types of costs.
